   "source_id": "H\\equiv U+PV",
   "kind": "exercise",
   "bloom": "Analyze",
   "place": {
    "at": "end"
   },
   "cite": "heat-at-constant-pressure",
   "prompt": "<p>Show that when $n$ moles of an ideal gas change temperature by $\\Delta T$ at constant pressure, $\\kDeltaH - \\kDeltaU = nR\\,\\Delta T$.</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "ai",
    "hint": "Write $\\kDeltaH$ from $\\kHenth \\equiv \\kU + \\kP\\kV$ and hold $\\kP$ fixed.",
    "solution": "<p>From $\\kHenth \\equiv \\kU + \\kP\\kV$, $\\kDeltaH = \\kDeltaU + \\Delta(\\kP\\kV)$. At constant pressure $\\Delta(\\kP\\kV) = \\kP\\,\\Delta\\kV$, so $\\kDeltaH - \\kDeltaU = \\kP\\,\\Delta\\kV$. For an ideal gas $\\kP\\kV = nRT$, and with $\\kP$ and $n$ fixed, $\\kP\\,\\Delta\\kV = nR\\,\\Delta T$. Hence $\\kDeltaH - \\kDeltaU = nR\\,\\Delta T$: the difference is the work the gas does pushing back its surroundings as it expands.</p>"
   }
  }
 ],
